The integration value of the OR node $$Z_i$$ is defined as

$$Z_i = Z_{A_1} \cup Z_{A_2}, \cdots, \cup Z_{A_n}$$

$$= \left( \begin{bmatrix} \max(\mu_{A_1}^L, \mu_{A_2}^L, \cdots, \mu_{A_n}^L), \\ \max(\mu_{A_1}^H, \mu_{A_2}^H, \cdots, \mu_{A_n}^H) \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} \min(\nu_{A_1}^L, \nu_{A_2}^L, \cdots, \nu_{A_n}^L), \\ \min(\nu_{A_1}^H, \nu_{A_2}^H, \cdots, \nu_{A_n}^H) \end{bmatrix} \right).$$
(13)

**d: THE OPERATION BETWEEN THE ATTACK NODES AND DEFENSE NODES**

Let $$Z_{A_i} = \left( \left[ \mu_{A_i}^L, \mu_{A_i}^H \right], \left[ \nu_{A_i}^L, \nu_{A_i}^H \right] \right)$$ represent the comprehensive value of one attack leaf node, and let $$Z_{D_i} = \left( \left[ \mu_{D_i}^L, \mu_{D_i}^H \right], \left[ \nu_{D_i}^L, \nu_{D_i}^H \right] \right)$$ represent the comprehensive value of one defense leaf node. Therefore, the operation between the attack nodes and defense nodes is defined

$$R_i = Z_{A_i} \otimes Z_{D_i}^c$$

$$= \left( \left[ \mu_{A_i}^L, \mu_{A_i}^H \right], \left[ \nu_{A_i}^L, \nu_{A_i}^H \right] \right) \otimes \left( \left[ \nu_{D_i}^L, \nu_{D_i}^H \right], \left[ \mu_{D_i}^L, \mu_{D_i}^H \right] \right)$$

$$= \left( \begin{bmatrix} \mu_{A_i}^L \nu_{D_i}^L, \mu_{A_i}^H \nu_{D_i}^H \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 1 - \left( 1 - \nu_{A_i}^L \right) \left( 1 - \mu_{D_i}^L \right), \\ 1 - \left( 1 - \nu_{A_i}^H \right) \left( 1 - \mu_{D_i}^H \right) \end{bmatrix} \right)$$
(14)

**2) INTEGRATION OF ATTACK PATHS**

Assume that $$X_{path} = \{X_1, X_2, \cdots, X_n\}$$ is the attack path, where $$X_t = \left( \left[ \mu_t^L, \mu_t^H \right], \left[ \nu_t^L, \nu_t^H \right] \right) (t = 1, 2, \cdots, m)$$.

The comprehensive value of the attack path is expressed as

$$Z_{X_{path}} = X_1 \otimes X_2 \otimes \cdots \otimes X_m$$

$$= \left( \begin{bmatrix} \prod_{t=1}^{m} \mu_t^L, \prod_{t=1}^{m} \mu_t^H \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 1 - \prod_{t=1}^{m} \left( 1 - \nu_t^L \right), 1 - \prod_{t=1}^{m} \left( 1 - \nu_t^H \right) \end{bmatrix} \right)$$
(15)

**3) DETERMINATION OF RISK SCORES**

Let $$p = \left( \left[ \mu_p^L, \mu_p^H \right], \left[ \nu_p^L, \nu_p^H \right] \right)$$ be the comprehensive value of any one attack path; and then the risk score of this attackpath is calculated by

$$S(p) = \frac{1}{2} \left[ \left( \mu_p^L + \mu_p^H \right) - \left( \nu_p^L + \nu_p^H \right) \right]$$
(16)

where $$S(p) \in [-1, 1]$$. The larger the value of $$S(p)$$, the higher the risk score of the attack path.

